Date: Mon, 5 Jul 93 23:07:51 WET DST
From: rjc@gnu.ai.mit.edu (Ray)
Subject: META:anonymity and list rules

nobody@rosebud.ee.uh.edu writes:


>If an open-minded, forward looking list like Extropians  shuts out
>anonymity, does it have much of a future?

   No one is shutting out anonymity, it is just that I don't have the
time to implement it. The software is somewhat object oriented, and
the specifications will be available soon so anyone who writes a
simple encryption/mail delivery module can add anonymous return.  I
elected to make certain tradeoffs during my implementation, and one of
them was to use the email address as a hash key for a database storing
per user settings.
  This means that all the users on the list must have unique email addresses,
hence no reforwarding. (keep in mind, that you can still post via
anonymous remailer, but to receive the post, you must have a registered
email address)

>If a violation of list rules was perpetrated by an anonymous poster, would
>complaining to the administrator of the remailer do any good? Could they
>trace it to the true name of the poster? If they could, would they?
 
 Harry would probably try to get the real address of the person
remailing. Most of the people running remailers _are_ extropians, or
friends of extropians. I'm sure we have influence. I don't relish the
though of having to track down such incidents.  We spent a week
tracking down all of pandit singh's aliases, and he ended up 
getting his accounts disabled. (mostly because Pandit hadn't
payed his net.bills, and being a net.asshole was the straw that broke the
his sysadmins back)

>Back on the original question of anonymous subscription, what if the list
>admin knew the identity of the subscriber, but kept no record of it other
>than an encrypted forwarding address?

  Again, the problem is, how is the list software supposed to
tell the difference between one nobody@rosebud and another? If you
want list features like kill files, ranked posting, reputation market, etc
it needs to be able to know who to send the result to and which database
record to operate on. I chose to use the email address to do this because
it saves the user from having to specify a handle/alias everytime
they want to do a command. If you want anonymous return, use a penet
type system, or implement your own mail agent when the specifications
are released.

  On the question of how I would catch a person reforwarding Extropian
email, I think I could probably do it. I have some stegnographic
ideas which would probably suffice. Since there are only 261 users
on the list, I need only to hide roughly 8bits of data in about
700k-1mb of data that goes out per week. So it could probably be
done, unless the person really put some effort into analyzing the msgs for
discrepencies.  I hope no one on the list is immature enough to
start a cryptowar. The last person who reforward messages 
(pandit singh) caused an inquisition and a new level of security
to be added for new users. For instance, Harry no longer allows new people to
have mail delivered to group aliases (e.g. if there are 10 people
at one college, have mail sent to extropian-students@foo-university.edu,
instead, each much subscribe individually)
Also, I run a script about once a month that calls up every internet
user on the list and checks his address for anything suspicous
(via SMTP expn <username>, look for pipes to mail2news, slocal, or
a group list)

  I'm not about to outlaw anonymity, in fact, I use PGP all the time, and am
on the cypherpunks list. It's just that I don't have the time to work on it
when there is more important software to get done. Even if we had a list
where everyone posted anonymously, we still would have to worry about
copyright fraud, such as someone publishing extropian list messages in
a book or selling them on a commercial site like compuserve.

(besides, I couldn't outlaw anonymity anyway. That's Harry's job,
communication director of ExI, and he's interested in adding
cryptography, like digicash reputation markets to the list)


-Ray


