Date: Tue, 6 Jul 93 10:17:03 CDT
From: derek@cs.wisc.edu (Derek Zahn)
Subject: Nightly Market Report

SLUGGISH TRADING AND MONETARY CRISIS IN THE HEX REP MARKET

Pardon if this is ignorant; one reason I want to participate
in the HEx market is that I want to learn more about markets
and how they work.  I see some grave difficulties with the
current situation on HEx.  Comments welcome.

Suppose there are 20 participants.  Each participant gets
10,000 Thornes, making the "liquid" money supply equal to
200,000p.  Each participant issues 10,000 shares, making
the total supply of shares equal to 200,000 also.  Now,
the initial trend seems to be for the asking price of
these new issues to average 50 or so.  This seems like
a strange and lopsided situation... I own 10,000 shares
of my own reputation, but am only able to buy about
200 shares of others' reputations (according to the
average asking price).  I suppose that ideally I'd buy
some shares of others which would give them money to buy
some shares of me which I could in turn reinvest in them...
but this process is likely to take a LONG time -- months
at least!  It seems to me that a good situation would be
to have reputation owners holding half of their own reps
(or so), which implies that -- given current pricing --
each person would be taking in and spending about 250,000p
to get to that point (5,000 * 50).  This will take many
cycles of trading.

In a real market, I guess that the prices might naturally
fall to lower levels, but that seems unlikely to happen
here for two reasons:

1) The system itself.  An average price of 2-5 seems like
   it might be more reasonable, but the granularity of the
   prices doesn't really allow trading in this range (2.5
   isn't allowed).

2) There is no real incentive for people to drop their prices,
   since all it does is reduce their perceived net worth.

SOLUTION:  I suggest the following radical step:  A drastic
inflationary move, quickly before it's too late.  A 100-1
multiplication of all funds, so that people start with
a million Thornes instead of just 10,000.

derek
