Date: Tue, 6 Jul 93 10:59:03 MDT
From: Mark_Muhlestein@Novell.COM (Mark Muhlestein)
Subject: Stephen Hawking lecture

This weekend I had the pleasure of attending a lecture given by
Stephen Hawking in Salt Lake City, Utah.  He was in town
overseeing the production of his new planetarium show, "Fates of
the Universe."  Of the 8000 people who came to see him, only
2800 of us got in, so I was glad we came early.  The lecture was
for a non-technical audience, was very witty and entertaining,
and was *very* warmly received.

After giving a general overview of black holes, (including his
"insurance" bet with Kip Thorne in which he had to buy a year's
subscription to "Penthouse" if Cygnus X-1 turned out to be a
black hole, with Thorne buying Hawking 5 years of "Private Eye"
as consolation if it wasn't), Hawking described some of his
latest research, on what he called "baby universes."

After discussing the idea that matter falling into a black hole
emerges from a white hole somewhere else in spacetime, he
considered the source of the energy emitted as Hawking radiation
(which he modestly avoided referring to by name).  As nearly as
I could understand, he said one could consider that two black
holes could be connected via a "baby universe" in which time was
"imaginary," or as he put it, runs at right angles to our time.
He seemed to be saying that matter falling into one black hole
could be emitted as Hawking radiation from the other one.

He stated that the existence of these baby universes was not
taken into account by superstring theory and could affect the
ability to make accurate predictions with that theory.  I am
guessing that he was saying that we might not be able to
accurately use superstring without taking quantum gravity
effects into account.  I had the impression that these baby
universes were connecting black holes which contained perhaps
only one or a few elementary particles, which would imply very
short lifetimes.

I'm sure the physics jocks on the list (maybe Mike Price could
comment) can give more information on this, or correct my
mistakes in understanding.

Anyway, it occurred to me that it will be a shame if a fine mind
like Hawking's is lost because of the current lack of medical
ability.  He would certainly be a prime candidate for cryonics,
it seems to me.

Mark_Muhlestein@novell.com

