Date: Tue, 6 Jul 93 10:49:31 -0700
From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: BOOK: "Replay," by Ken Grimwood

"Replay," by Ken Grimwood, is several years old now (1986) and may be old
news to many of you. I read it for the first time on Sunday, based on a
hearty recommendation by Jay Freeman. I also heartily recommend it.

It's sometimes called "fantasy," but I think in many ways it's the "hardest
of the hard" SF, if viewed in terms of time travel, pocket universes,
strange loops in the flow of time, or whatever. The plot touches on several
oft-discussed themes of our List, such as "the universe as a simulation,"
the reality of multiple universes, and the nature of time.

The main character finds himself dying on the same date (in 1988) and then
immediately finding himself back in his college days, circa 1963, where he
once again lives his life. Each new life is different, as he can use his
knowledge of future events (who wins the Kentucky Derby in 1963 is his
first chance to make a lot of money) to amass incredible wealth. He
realizes soon enough that he will die at the precise time in 1988, no
matter what he does.

So he sets out to use his multiple lives to accomplish various things.

(This caters to my "time travel fantasy" while neatly sidestepping time
travel paradoxes. Each universe is different, that is, he cannot interfere
with his own past.)

The character development is good, and the evocation of the 1960s and 70s
is interesting.

Some exciting plot twists (hint: the appearance of the Spielberg-Lucas
movie "Starsea") liven up the pace and suggest strange possibilities. I
couldn't put the book down, even to go out and watch some fireworks on the
beach here.

Strongly recommended.


-Tim May
