Date: Tue, 6 Jul 93 19:59:02 MDT
From: Mark_Muhlestein@Novell.COM (Mark Muhlestein)
Subject: NANO: Drexler style nanotech is unworkable

This exchange has given me the excuse I needed to dig back into
_Nanosystems_ again, which has been worthwhile for me, and I hope for
others.  James is right to object to Drexler's work when it conflicts
with what he knows; let's try to understand things better together.

I wrote, quoting _Nanosystems_:

>>      Design and analysis have shown, however, that dry bearings
>>      with atomically precise surfaces need not suffer these
>>      problems.  As shown in Chapters 6, 7, and 10, dynamic
>>      friction can be low, and both static friction and wear can
>>      be made negligible.

To which James responded:

> His claim that wear can be made negligible, or truly zero,
> with atomically precise surface is of course true
>
> His claim that static friction can be made low, is totally
> unsupported. He simply makes bland assertions about van der
> Waals forces.

Well, it is unsupported in that quote, but it is "shown in Chapters
6, 7, and 10."  If you want me to take you seriously, I want to
see evidence that you have read and understood those chapters.

> Furthermore he makes a flagrant error.  He assumes that
> thermal motion will overcome any binding due to the van der
> Waals forces, as it does in biological systems.

I do not see evidence of such an assumption.  You, on the other hand,
appear to be making the assumption that Drexler has not already
considered binding due to van der Waals forces.  But, (_Nanosystems_
p. 66):

     Chapters 7 and 10 examine the mechanical properties of
     sliding, unreactive, atomically precise interfaces in which
     van der Waals attraction and overlap repulsion are the
     dominant forces. [footnote: This is not universal: metal
     surfaces (for example) would undergo bonding rather than
     repulsion; potential functions are discussed in
     Israelachvili (1992).] This requires a model for the
     interaction energy of two such surfaces at small
     separations.  In the interfaces of greatest practical
     interest, the structures of the two surfaces are out of
     register (owing to differences in lattice spacing or to a
     relative rotation).
	  
Drexler goes on to give the (ASCII-unfriendly) formula for plane-plane
interaction energy potential, including van der Waals and overlap
repulsion effects.

James, please read chapters 7 and 10, and explain where Drexler
goes wrong.  I'm not saying he's not wrong, I would just like you
to explain it in terms of the data presented there.  I am growing
frustrated with assertions such as:

> This assumption can only be true if the nanomachine has
> substantial internal random degrees of freedom, but the
> whole point of a Drexler design is to avoid this.  He
> assumes that which he denies.

when it seems likely that you don't have a clear idea of just
what it is Drexler is assuming (or denying).

> The "analog" of static friction, will have results exactly same as
> static friction, unless the entropy of the random motion of many
> internal degrees of freedom of the nanomachine is greater than the
> energy barrier.  Drexler needs to evaluate roughly how large this
> "analog" is going to be.  He makes not attempt to do so, perhaps
> because there is every reason to expect it to be very large for large
> nanomachines. containing many tightly linked parts.

Why do you say he makes no attempt to evaluate this?  As a matter of
fact, calculations of energy barriers and other such evaluations make
up the bulk of the book.  This is why I have trouble believing that you
have really read and understood _Nanosystems_.

>       its closest analogues -- energy barriers
>       and force gradients  ...-- can be lower for two coupled objects
>       sliding over a surface than for either object considered
>       separately (Section 10.8) even though their contact forces
>       are additive. 
> Consider two randomly oriented graphite planes, as in highly oriented
> pyrolytic graphite.  By Drexler's arguments (10.8) there should be
> systematic cancellation in the energy barrier against one layer sliding
> over the other because the two planes are randomly oriented, so the
> layers should easily slide.

The point about the lower energy barrier of coupled objects serves to
illustrate only one difference with static friction.  Even so, your
example seems to be off the mark.  Are the HOPG planes atomically
precise and perfectly flat?  If not, graphite's unsaturated tetravalent
atoms can react under a compressive load of ~0.5 nN per atom (p. 297).

Please read (or re-read) the relevant sections of _Nanosystems_ before
pronouncing again on what Drexler argues.  The opening sections of
chapter 10 describe in great detail the assumptions and computational
procedures used in the analysis of moving nano components.  It is very
misleading to characterize them as "naive arguments that treat van der
Waals forces as pairwise independent."

> Molecules analogous to those proposed by Drexler exist.  Their behavior
> is roughly what you would expect by scaling.  Nanomachines will suffer
> the same problems as micromachines do, only more so.

If you can produce any of the molecular machine designs proposed in
chapter 10 of _Nanosystems_, I'm sure the list would kick in to buy
your ticket to Stockholm (insert smiley as needed).

Also, I have the impression that you assume that Drexler ignores all
problems in his analyses.  Chapter 7 ends with this paragraph:

     _Some open problems._  Many of these mechanisms are
     associated with open problems.  The theory of phonon
     transmission through sliding interfaces, in particular, is
     challenging and in need of further work.  For example, in
     the regime of lowest drag -- at separations where the mean
     interfacial stiffness goes to zero -- the phonon
     transmission model described here becomes unrealistic.
     Further, the interfaces of greatest engineering interest
     are not between identical half-spaces; they may be curved,
     or juxtapose dissimilar materials, or couple a small
     component to [a] large environment.  Finally, drag
     resulting from interfacial phonon-phonon scattering
     deserves a thorough treatment.  A survey of the devices
     discussed in Chapters 10-14 reveals many situations of
     practical interest for which the models presented in this
     chapter provide only rough estimates of energy
     dissipation.  In addition, many of these models can be
     improved by means of better approximations or more thorough
     analysis.

James, it sounds like you have interest and abilities in this area.  If
you are wrong, and Drexler-style nanotech _is_ workable, why not find
that out as soon as possible, so we can get to work on some of these
problems.  I believe we need something like nanotech to create the future
we all want.

